  "function": {
    "uniprot_function": "AXIN1 is a scaffold protein that forms the core of the β-catenin destruction complex, promoting phosphorylation of β-catenin by CK1α and GSK3β, leading to its ubiquitination and proteasomal degradation. Functions as a negative regulator of the Wnt signaling pathway. Also participates in other signaling pathways including Hippo, TGFβ, AMPK, mTOR, MAPK, and antioxidant signaling. Forms complexes with HIPK2 and TP53 to regulate apoptosis. Protects GSK3β from inhibitory phosphorylation by other kinases through allosteric mechanisms.",
    "catalytic_activity": "No intrinsic enzymatic activity. Functions as a scaffold protein organizing kinases (CK1α, GSK3β) and their substrates (β-catenin, APC, etc.) to enable processive phosphorylation",
    "subcellular_location": [
      "Cytoplasm",
      "Cytosol",
      "Nucleus",
      "β-catenin destruction complex",
      "Lateral plasma membrane",
      "Perinuclear region",
      "Cell membrane (upon Wnt stimulation and DVL/LRP5/6 binding)"
    ],
    "tissue_specificity": "Broadly expressed across all tissues with low tissue specificity (Tau score: 0.23). Ubiquitously expressed but functionality varies in different tissue contexts",
