    "pathway_role_summary": "AXIN1 is the core scaffold of the β-catenin destruction complex in canonical Wnt signaling. In the absence of Wnt ligands, AXIN1 organizes CK1α, GSK3β, APC, and β-catenin into a complex that promotes sequential phosphorylation of β-catenin, leading to its ubiquitination by β-TrCP and proteasomal degradation. Upon Wnt stimulation, AXIN1 is recruited to the membrane-bound LRP5/6-FZD-DVL complex, disrupting the destruction complex and allowing β-catenin stabilization. AXIN1 also participates in Hippo signaling and regulates apoptosis through TP53-HIPK2 complexes. Mutations or degradation of AXIN1 lead to constitutive Wnt activation and are implicated in hepatocellular carcinoma and other cancers."
  },
  "disease_associations": [
    {
      "disease": "Hepatocellular carcinoma",
      "omim_id": "114550",
      "mutation_type": "Somatic",
      "description": "AXIN1 mutations occur in approximately 10% of hepatocellular carcinomas. Mutations include a 33-bp deletion in exon 3 affecting GSK3β phosphorylation sites and 12 missense mutations. All mutations are somatic and lead to impaired β-catenin degradation and Wnt pathway activation. AXIN1-mutant HCC represents a molecularly distinct poor-prognosis subtype with lower Wnt activation than CTNNB1-mutant HCC"
    },
    {
      "disease": "Colorectal cancer",
      "omim_id": null,
      "mutation_type": "Somatic",
      "description": "AXIN1 mutations and loss of expression contribute to colorectal tumorigenesis through Wnt pathway activation. AXIN1 functions as a tumor suppressor"
    },
    {
      "disease": "Craniometadiaphyseal osteosclerosis with hip dysplasia",
